13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: The closed CVC core is the right way to read dad/dab/gut/low/cat and the five explicit negatives are built to break it.

dad d a d, dab d a b, gut g u t, low l o w, cat c a t are all monosyllabic CVC with V2 ∈ {a,o,u} and C3 ∈ {b,d,t,w}. Onset varies freely d,d,g,l,c and the whole positive set is e-free in spelling.

The negatives make the diagnostic explicit: ear/ego/ate bring in e and break CVC, cop is CVC with C3=p, mom is CVC with C3=m. Three of the five negatives are e-driven, e is the headline break.

On A–E

* rat = r a t is CVC with V2=a ∈ {a,o,u} and C3=t ∈ {b,d,t,w}. It is the natural a…t continuation of cat/dad/dab. Onset is already free, so a new onset r does not break the family.
* dam = d a m is e-free CVC, V2=a ok, fails only on C3=m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* car = c a r is e-free CVC, V2=a ok, fails only on C3=r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* bin = b i n is e-free CVC but V2=i ∉ {a,o,u} and C3=n ∉ {b,d,t,w}

leg = l e g is CVC in shape but V2=e and the word contains the letter e. No exemplar has an e nucleus or the letter e at all, and e is the defining feature of ear/ego/ate in the negative list. C3=g is also outside {b,d,t,w}.

Dam, car and bin all deviate on secondary coda or nucleus restrictions, but they stay e-free. Rat keeps the V2/C3 core of the family. The single most salient, repeated break with the positive set and with the negative exemplars ear/ego/ate is the introduction of an e nucleus/letter into an otherwise CVC shape. That is leg, the textbook non-member of the dad/dab/gut/low/cat class.

